  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nTonight through Tuesday:\n\nUnseasonable warmth will continue to be the story for a few more\ndays, particularly on Monday. Tonight, given dew points this\nafternoon in the mid to upper 50s in most spots, light \nsoutherly winds will enable overnight lows should to fall into \nthe upper 50s to lower 60s (locally mid 50s) away from Chicago.\nThese readings are still 10-15F above average for the date,\nproviding a very mild starting point for Monday's warmth.\n\nSome high cloud cover to start the day will give way to plenty\nof sunshine through the afternoon. Forecast soundings support\nsouthwesterly winds gusting up to 25 to 30 mph. With recent\ndryness and 925 mb temps in the lower to mid 20s Celsius, the\nsetup looks prime for temps overperforming, reaching the mid to\nupper 80s. Record highs for October 12th are relatively low\ncompared to surrounding days, making record highs a decent bet\nat Chicago and Rockford (see climate section below). \n\nCan't completely rule out some high based sprinkles in spots on\nMonday night in advance of an approaching cold front trailing \nfrom surface low pressure over northern Lake Michigan by early \nTuesday. The cold front will otherwise move across the area \nwith little fanfare on Tuesday. Exceptionally mild conditions\n(60s to around 70F) to start Tuesday and very weak cold\nadvection will result in another day of summer-like temps,\nthough not quite as warm as those on Monday.\n\nTuesday Night through Sunday:\n\nAnafrontal showers and some embedded thunderstorms are expected\nat times later Tuesday evening into Wednesday morning. This will\noccur as impulses riding fairly robust west-southwest mid-level\nflow interact with moisture and modest elevated instability \natop the baroclinic zone over the region. There's some\nuncertainty with specifics in this period, including the\npotential for axes of higher QPF. All in all, the fairly high \nPoPs in the official forecast appear warranted. High pressure\nbuilding down from the northern Great Lakes will shift showers\nprimarily south of I-80 by mid Wednesday morning. Any \nadditional afternoon showers will probably be confined to mainly\nsouth of the Illinois and Kankakee River Valleys (and perhaps\neven primarily south of US-24). Highs on Wednesday, while still\nabove average for the date, will be noticeably cooler than\nTuesday, particularly along the lakefront (mid-upper 60s) due to\nsteady northeast winds.\n\nThe next period of interest for showers and embedded\nthunderstorms is on Thursday. While there is considerable \nvariance in the evolution of key synoptic features on Thursday,\nincluding the track of weak surface low pressure, 60-70% PoPs\nin the official forecast appear reasonable. A strong cold front\nwill sweep across the area Thursday night, with the coolest air\nmass of the season thus far moving in behind the front for\nFriday-Saturday. Seasonably strong (1030s to around 1040 mb)\nhigh pressure transiting the region may set the stage for areas\nof frost outside of Chicago Friday night into early Saturday and\nSaturday night into early Sunday, depending on how cool surface \ntemps actually get. Expect temps to quickly moderate back to \nnear seasonable Sunday afternoon.\n\nCastro",
